Phase 1: Requirement Gathering and Analysis
Understand business requirements and data flows.
Identify source systems, target systems, and data volumes.
Gather source-to-target mapping documents.
Define data refresh frequency, SLAs, and dependencies.
Identify data quality rules and transformation requirements.

Phase 2: Architecture and Environment Setup
Design the ADF architecture and data ingestion strategy.
Create development, test, and production environments.
Configure Azure Data Factory and Azure integration resources.
Set up Azure Data Lake Storage, Azure SQL Database, or other targets.
Configure managed identities, RBAC, Key Vault, and networking.
Select integration runtime types and connectivity methods.

Phase 3: Source and Target Connectivity
Create linked services for source and target systems.
Configure datasets and connection parameters.
Establish connectivity to databases, files, APIs, and cloud storage.
Test authentication, permissions, and network access.
Parameterize connections where appropriate.

Phase 4: Pipeline Development
Develop ingestion pipelines using Copy Activity.
Build full-load and incremental-load pipelines.
Implement watermark-based or change-data-capture ingestion where supported.
Create reusable, metadata-driven pipelines.
Implement Lookup, ForEach, If Condition, Switch, and Execute Pipeline activities.
Configure triggers, schedules, dependencies, and retries.
Implement logging, error handling, and alerts.

Phase 5: Data Transformation
Implement transformations using Mapping Data Flows, SQL, Databricks, or other processing services as required.
Apply cleansing, filtering, joins, aggregations, and business rules.
Handle null values, duplicates, and invalid records.
Implement schema validation and reject handling.
Optimize transformation performance and data partitioning.

Phase 6: Testing and Validation
Perform unit testing of individual pipelines.
Validate source-to-target record counts and data accuracy.
Test incremental loads, reruns, and duplicate prevention.
Perform integration and end-to-end testing.
Test failure scenarios, retries, and recovery procedures.
Conduct performance testing and business user acceptance testing (UAT).

Phase 7: Deployment and Release Management
Integrate ADF with Azure DevOps or GitHub.
Maintain development branches and pull-request reviews.
Configure CI/CD deployment pipelines.
Deploy ADF resources using supported ARM templates or deployment mechanisms.
Manage environment-specific parameters and secrets.
Validate production connections, triggers, and permissions.
Prepare rollback and release plans.

Phase 8: Monitoring and Production Support
Monitor pipeline execution and activity run history.
Configure Azure Monitor alerts and diagnostic logging.
Troubleshoot failed activities and connectivity issues.
Reprocess failed data and manage pipeline reruns.
Monitor throughput, execution duration, and resource costs.

